@@ -102,4 +126,7 @@
 Out-of-Core ZSWEEP is a direct volume rendering technique for large unstructured grids that extends the in-core ZSWEEP algorithm to handle datasets exceeding available RAM by processing data in tiles and loading meta-cells from disk only when needed. It differs from its sibling [MIR](1281-mir.md) by being substantially faster and leveraging the ZSWEEP optimization of front-to-back sorting, at the cost of more complex preprocessing and higher main-memory requirements.
 
+### X-Bucket and X-Actives Processing
+This variant processes scanlines from bottom to top [S4]. For each scanline, a critical section of code is entered, allowing only one processor to run it at a time [S4]. The surviving polygons of the previous pixel's x-actives list are updated with newly interpolated field data and screen-z values, requiring rearrangement among survivors to maintain screen-z order [S4]. Taking into account the line-of-sight distance between adjacent polygons, the color-opacity contribution for the inter-polygon region is calculated and composited into a software floating-point frame buffer using standard equations [S4].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** Up to 5 times faster than the best previously-known exact algorithms that use comparable amounts of memory [S2, unofficial].
